WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.008 --> 00:00:08.468
Young Eva Duarte wanted to be famous. So she left home in her teens and worked hard

2
00:00:08.469 --> 00:00:14.905
to advance her acting career. But she only achieved true stardom after marrying a man who would become

3
00:00:14.906 --> 00:00:21.601
the president of Argentina. Eva’s work as first lady made her one of the most famous&#8212;and most

4
00:00:21.602 --> 00:00:30.611
controversial&#8212;women in the world. Born in Los Toldos, Argentina, on May 7, 1919, Eva was soon nicknamed

5
00:00:30.612 --> 00:00:38.873
Evita, "little Eva." Her father was a wealthy landowner, but Evita and her four older siblings were shunned

6
00:00:38.874 --> 00:00:50.155
by the neighbors because their parents were not married. After Evita’s father died in a car accident

7
00:00:50.156 --> 00:00:55.925
in 1926, she, her mother, and her sisters worked as seamstresses and cooks for wealthy households to

8
00:00:55.926 --> 00:01:04.089
pay the bills. As a result, Evita learned of the vast differences between classes in Argentina. She later

9
00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:12.796
said, "I was very sad for many days when I discovered that in the world there were

10
00:01:12.797 --> 00:01:19.795
poor people and rich people." Soon the family moved to a larger town called Jun&iacute;n, where her

11
00:01:19.796 --> 00:01:28.232
mother opened a boardinghouse. Life became easier for Evita. She regularly attended school and found she loved reciting

12
00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:34.897
poetry. She also loved the movies. These two passions merged when she had the chance to act

13
00:01:34.898 --> 00:01:40.326
in school plays. Evita decided to become a famous movie star. At age 15 she packed her

14
00:01:40.327 --> 00:01:44.993
bags and moved to Argentina’s biggest city&#8212;Buenos Aires. She worked as a model and got her first role

15
00:01:44.994 --> 00:01:50.374
on stage in March 1935. After that she toured with a theater company, performing small parts. Soon

16
00:01:50.375 --> 00:01:57.024
Evita’s picture began appearing in local newspapers and magazines. In 1942 she got her big break when

17
00:01:57.025 --> 00:02:02.038
the owner of the Radical Soap Company noticed her. He cast Evita as the star in a radio

18
00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:08.402
show the company sponsored. Some listeners thought she was a bad actress, but others said she portrayed

19
00:02:08.403 --> 00:02:12.521
suffering women well. Almost all who knew her agreed she worked very hard. The head of the

20
00:02:12.522 --> 00:02:22.402
largest radio company in the country soon gave her the leading role in a new program, playing famous

21
00:02:22.403 --> 00:02:27.355
women such as Elizabeth I of England. She became one of the busiest and highest-paid radio stars

22
00:02:27.356 --> 00:02:34.678
in Argentina. On January 22, 1944, Evita attended a benefit for Argentine earthquake victims. On this "marvelous

23
00:02:34.679 --> 00:02:42.011
day," as she called it, Evita met Colonel Juan Per&oacute;n, a 48-year-old widower. He was drawn to the

24
00:02:42.012 --> 00:02:46.983
pretty, 24-year-old actress, and they started seeing each other. In the mid-1940s, the Argentine government was in

25
00:02:46.984 --> 00:02:51.610
turmoil. The president resigned in February 1944, and Juan became vice president, minister of war, and secretary

26
00:02:51.611 --> 00:03:00.439
of labor. Some people feared that all these positions made Juan too powerful, and they forced him to

27
00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:12.762
resign on October 9, 1945. Within days they went even further and imprisoned him. Argentina’s workers did

28
00:03:12.763 --> 00:03:18.906
not stand for that; thousands demonstrated for Juan’s freedom. Some people claim that Evita rallied the protesters;

29
00:03:18.907 --> 00:03:28.790
others argue that she was not involved. Either way, Juan was released on October 17. Days later he

30
00:03:28.791 --> 00:03:34.393
married Evita, and they started campaigning for the presidency together. Evita was the first wife of a

31
00:03:34.394 --> 00:03:39.407
presidential candidate to give speeches. She told the poor that she understood them, saying, "I speak in

32
00:03:39.408 --> 00:03:49.895
the name of the humble, the homeless, to cry out against the old, evil days." Excited about a

33
00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:58.056
leader who supported the workers instead of the rich, the crowds chanted, "Evita, Evita!" With Evita’s help,

34
00:03:58.057 --> 00:04:03.769
Juan became president on June 4, 1946. Evita was a new kind of first lady. Although she had

35
00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:14.280
no official title, she became an active member of the government, with an office in the ministry

36
00:04:14.281 --> 00:04:24.408
of labor. Not everyone approved of her involvement. When she came to a government meeting that was

37
00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:30.241
normally attended only by elected officials, a senator objected to her presence. He learned a valuable lesson&#8212;those who

38
00:04:30.242 --> 00:04:38.257
offended Evita paid a high price. He was tossed out of the government and jailed. Evita also

39
00:04:38.258 --> 00:04:50.079
traveled to Europe to meet the pope and the heads of many countries. At receptions she glistened

40
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:55.787
in jewels and designer dresses. But many Europeans were unsure if they could trust this woman, who claimed

41
00:04:55.788 --> 00:05:00.113
to love the poor but dressed like a queen. She upset many people by visiting Spain, a

42
00:05:00.114 --> 00:05:07.609
country ruled by a fascist dictator. Some demonstrators in Europe even threw stones and tomatoes at her.

43
00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:14.991
In Argentina Evita was not popular with everyone, but she continued to work hard for what she wanted.

44
00:05:14.992 --> 00:05:22.419
She annoyed many men by insisting that women be given the right to vote, which was granted

45
00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:27.373
in 1947. She disturbed the upper class by supporting the workers, saying, "The land belongs to him

46
00:05:27.374 --> 00:05:33.365
who works it." Evita knew that her power came from the devotion of the lower classes. Workers loved

47
00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:40.969
her and called her the "Queen of Labor" and the "Lady of Hope." She organized the Eva

48
00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:45.080
Per&oacute;n Foundation, which funded housing for the poor, the elderly, and single mothers. It also built schools,

49
00:05:45.081 --> 00:05:50.830
orphanages, and hospitals. Evita solicited money for her foundation from big companies and rich individuals. Most donated, but

50
00:05:50.831 --> 00:05:55.504
some objected&#8212;for which they were punished. A candy company that resisted giving money to the foundation was

51
00:05:55.505 --> 00:06:01.230
closed for three years. Newspapers that carried negative stories about Evita were also shut down, and her

52
00:06:01.231 --> 00:06:10.045
critics were spied upon and had their telephones tapped. She was still beloved by the workers, however, and

53
00:06:10.046 --> 00:06:17.776
they urged her to run for vice president in the election of 1951. She declined, probably because

54
00:06:17.777 --> 00:06:22.554
she knew by then that she was sick. Evita had cancer, but she voted for her husband

55
00:06:22.555 --> 00:06:30.586
from her hospital bed in the first election in which women could participate. Evita’s last public appearance was

56
00:06:30.587 --> 00:06:46.097
on June 4, 1952, at Juan’s inauguration, where she was given the title "Spiritual Leader of the

57
00:06:46.098 --> 00:06:54.412
Nation." She died on July 26, 1952, at age 33. Evita’s death led to an enormous outpouring

58
00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:59.341
of grief. Two million Argentines came to see her lying in state. Flowers carpeted the streets and businesses

59
00:06:59.342 --> 00:07:04.941
closed for three days. Juan had her body embalmed and planned to display it in an elaborate

60
00:07:04.942 --> 00:07:11.118
shrine. But he was ousted as president in 1955, before the monument could be finished. The new

61
00:07:11.119 --> 00:07:18.332
military government worried that Evita’s death would encourage her admirers to take up the causes she supported. So

62
00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:22.109
they hid her body in Italy for over a decade. In the 1970s Juan returned to the

63
00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:33.717
presidency, and Evita was finally buried in Argentina in 1976. Today many people visit her grave. Evita

64
00:07:33.718 --> 00:07:43.428
sought fame her entire life. Beloved by some and feared by others, she became an important part of

65
00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:50.261
Argentina’s history. She said, "I left my dreams by the wayside in order to watch over the

66
00:07:50.262 --> 00:07:55.753
dreams of others." Did she? Even today people cannot agree about this former first lady. Was she

67
00:07:55.754 --> 00:08:02.682
truly a champion of the poor or was she simply acting the part to advance her own career?

68
00:08:02.683 --> 00:08:09.416
No one will ever know, but few would argue that she achieved fame beyond her wildest hopes.

